Ladybird’s UI process now opens a local file for a renderer only under
the user’s home directory, the temporary directories, and mounted
volumes, once every symbolic link in the path is resolved, and never
anything under /proc, /sys, or /dev. File: navigations read through the
same check, whoever started them.

That allow list is close to ChromeOS’s: IsAccessAllowedChromeOS() in
chrome/browser/net/chrome_network_delegate.cc allows only the user’s
files (MyFiles), removable media (/media), the temporary directory, and
a few system log and asset directories, and checks both the path and
its absolute form. Desktop Chrome (IsAccessAllowed() allows any path
there, and its FileURLLoader streams the bytes rather than handing over
a descriptor) and Firefox (whose file:// content process gets a
read-only tree of “/” in SandboxBrokerPolicyFactory) allow every
path, so a desktop Ladybird is stricter than either.

This comparison comes from reading the engines’ sources only.
